{0 A first CLI}

This tutorial writes [check], a command that runs test suites, shows them as
progress rows while they run and prints a table of their results. It takes a
[--color] option and a [--theme] option, prints plain lines when its output
is a pipe, and has a cram test whose output is the same on every machine.
Each section adds to the one before it.

{1:setup Setup}

[check] uses the [console] and [console-eio] packages, with
{{:https://erratique.ch/software/cmdliner}Cmdliner} for its options and
{{:https://erratique.ch/software/logs}Logs} for its messages:

{v
opam install console console-eio cmdliner logs
v}

Its [dune] file:

{v
(executable
 (public_name check)
 (libraries
  console console.tty console-eio
  eio eio_main fmt fmt.cli cmdliner logs))
v}

{1:options Colour and theme}

A program decides on colour once, before it prints. {!Console_tty.setup}
reads the terminal and the environment ([NO_COLOR], [TERM], [COLORTERM] and
the locale) and sets colour, colour depth and UTF-8 on the standard outputs.
Given a style renderer, it uses that one instead: [Fmt_cli.style_renderer]
reads it from a [--color=auto|always|never] option. A
{{!Console.Theme}theme} is kept on the formatter too, and the progress rows
draw on standard error, so [check] sets the theme of both outputs:

{@ocaml[
let setup color theme =
  Console_tty.setup ?style_renderer:color ();
  List.iter (fun ppf -> Console.Theme.set ppf theme) [ Fmt.stdout; Fmt.stderr ];
  Logs.set_level (Some Logs.Info)

let theme =
  Cmdliner.Arg.(
    value
    & opt (enum Console.Theme.all) Console.Theme.default
    & info [ "theme" ] ~doc:"The look of the output.")

let options = Cmdliner.Term.(const setup $ Fmt_cli.style_renderer () $ theme)
]}

{!Console.Theme.all} lists the predefined themes under their names, which are
the values [--theme] accepts:

{@ocaml[
# List.map fst Console.Theme.all;;
- : string list =
["default"; "ascii"; "dos"; "matrix"; "rainbow"; "dracula"; "nord";
 "solarized-dark"; "solarized-light"; "gruvbox-dark"; "gruvbox-light";
 "catppuccin-latte"; "catppuccin-mocha"; "tokyo-night"; "one-dark";
 "monokai"; "github-dark"; "github-light"]
]}

{!Console.Theme.default} reads on any background. A theme named after a
colour scheme, such as [dracula], is for a terminal that uses that scheme.

{1:table A table}

A table is a list of columns and rows of {{!Console.Span}spans}. It is as
wide as its content needs, and no wider than the formatter's margin. Its
frame, the style of its headers and its colours come from the theme of the
formatter it is printed on:

{@ocaml[
# let suites = [ ("parser", 3); ("layout", 2) ];;
val suites : (string * int) list = [("parser", 3); ("layout", 2)]
# let results suites =
    let row (name, n) =
      [ Console.Span.text name; Console.Span.text (Int.to_string n) ]
    in
    Console.Table.(
      v [ column "Suite"; column ~align:`Right "Tests" ] (List.map row suites));;
val results : (string * int) list -> Console.Table.t = <fun>
# Fmt.pr "%a@." Console.Table.pp (results suites);;
╭────────┬───────╮
│ Suite  │ Tests │
├────────┼───────┤
│ parser │     3 │
│ layout │     2 │
╰────────┴───────╯
- : unit = ()
]}

{1:progress Progress rows}

{!Console_eio.run} opens a display on standard error, passes it to a
function and closes it when the function returns. {!Console.Display.with_task}
runs a function as a task: its row shows while the function runs, and the
task succeeds when the function returns and fails when it raises.
{!Console.Display.set_count} says how much of the work is done:

{@ocaml[
let test display (name, total) =
  Console.Display.with_task display name @@ fun task ->
  for cur = 1 to total do
    Console.Display.set_count task ~cur ~total
  done;
  Logs.info (fun m -> m "%d tests passed" total)

let run_all ~clock suites =
  Console_eio.run ~clock ~header:"Testing" (fun display ->
      Eio.Fiber.List.iter (test display) suites)
]}

[Eio.Fiber.List.iter] runs each suite in a fiber of its own, so suites that
wait, on a child process say, run side by side, one row each. A [Logs]
message logged inside a task, as here, goes to the task's window under its
row while it runs and then stays, after the task's name. The output of a
command the task runs goes to {!Console.Display.log} instead: it shows in the
window while the task runs, goes when the task succeeds and is printed whole
when it fails. What the program prints on standard output with [Fmt.pr] is
its result, which console prints above the rows and never hides. The section
{{!Console_eio.output}where text goes} has the whole table.

{1:program The whole program}

[check] sets up the outputs, runs the suites, then prints the table:

{@ocaml[
let check () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  run_all ~clock:(Eio.Stdenv.mono_clock env) suites;
  Fmt.pr "%a@." Console.Table.pp (results suites)

let cmd =
  Cmdliner.Cmd.v (Cmdliner.Cmd.info "check")
    Cmdliner.Term.(const check $ options)

let main () = exit (Cmdliner.Cmd.eval cmd)
]}

and its entry point is [let () = main ()].

{1:pipe What a pipe gets}

When the output is not a terminal, as in a pipe or a CI log, nothing is
redrawn. A task prints a line when it starts, each of its messages after its
name, and one when it ends, with its count and its time; the header comes
last, as a summary. Without [--color=always], a pipe gets no colour. Here
[check] runs with its output captured, and with [CONSOLE_FROZEN_CLOCK] set,
which the {{!testing}next section} explains:

{@ocaml[
# Unix.putenv "CONSOLE_FROZEN_CLOCK" "1";;
- : unit = ()
# Cmdliner.Cmd.eval ~argv:[| "check"; "--theme=dos" |] cmd;;
[-] parser
  parser: 3 tests passed
[+] parser  3/3  0.0s
[-] layout
  layout: 2 tests passed
[+] layout  2/2  0.0s
[+] Testing 0.0s (2/2)
╔════════╤═══════╗
║ Suite  │ Tests ║
╟────────┼───────╢
║ parser │     3 ║
║ layout │     2 ║
╚════════╧═══════╝
- : int = 0
]}

The marks come from the theme, here {!Console.Theme.dos}. Console flushes
standard output before each line it writes, so [check 2>&1 | cat] keeps the
order of the two outputs. A [--progress=plain] option can ask for these lines
on a terminal too, with [Console_eio.run ~plain:true].

{1:testing Testing the output}

An elapsed time is the one part of the output that the machine writes rather
than the program. With [CONSOLE_FROZEN_CLOCK] set to any value but the empty
string, {!Console_eio.run} reads a clock stopped at zero: every time reads
[0.0s], no rate shows, and the spinners and the motion stand still. A cram
test of [check] then reads the same on every machine. Its [dune] stanza:

{v
(cram
 (deps %{bin:check}))
v}

and its file, [check.t]:

{v
  $ export CONSOLE_FROZEN_CLOCK=1
  $ check 2>&1
  • parser
    parser: 3 tests passed
  ✓ parser  3/3  0.0s
  • layout
    layout: 2 tests passed
  ✓ layout  2/2  0.0s
  ✓ Testing 0.0s (2/2)
  ╭────────┬───────╮
  │ Suite  │ Tests │
  ├────────┼───────┤
  │ parser │     3 │
  │ layout │     2 │
  ╰────────┴───────╯
v}

The widgets need no clock: [Console.Table.to_string] and the other
[to_string] functions give the text a unit test compares, plain unless
[~styled:true].

